Scott’s Proposition 6.7 says that if \(i : T(D) \to D\) is an initial \(T\)-algebra, then \(T(i) : T^2(D) \to T(D)\) is an initial \(T\)-algebra and \(i\) is an isomorphism from \(T(D)\) onto \(D\). The printed argument first notes that functoriality makes \((T(D), T(i))\) a \(T\)-algebra, takes the unique homomorphism \(j\) from the initial algebra \((D, i)\) into that algebra, observes that \(i\) itself is a homomorphism the other way, and concludes \(i \circ j = I_D\) because an endomorphism of an initial algebra is the identity; functoriality then gives \(T(i) \circ T(j) = I_{T(D)}\), and the homomorphism square for \(j\) gives \(j \circ i = I_{T(D)}\). The gloss of `theorem_67` establishes only the first of those identities: the structure map, composed with the descent homomorphism into the algebra obtained by applying \(T\) to the structure map, equals the identity on the carrier (`str_comp_desc`). It does not export the reverse composite, the resulting isomorphism, or initiality of \(T(i)\), and the printed proof likewise never shows that \(T(i)\) is initial. **Theorem:** partial — `theorem_67` is the identity \(i \circ j = I_D\), not both inverse laws and not initiality of \(T(i)\). **Proof:** partial — uniqueness of the endomorphism matches the step \(i \circ j = I_D\); the functoriality step and the homomorphism-square step \(j \circ i = I_{T(D)}\) are absent from the numbered theorem.
